import{_ as i,P as a,d as n,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.22 Class, Identity, Inequality, and Social Exclusion","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.22 Class, Identity, Inequality, and Social Exclusion","xkChars":0,"xkWords":446,"xkLessons":{"D.22":{"title":"D.22 Class, Identity, Inequality, and Social Exclusion","lead":"Income, wealth, control, and recognition are not the same resource. Group statistics can be true without directly diagnosing a member's circumstances or explaining the causes of differences.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4957–4972, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-22#section-d-22. See the theory-v2 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘A person's circumstances cannot be diagnosed directly from an identity category.’ Income is a flow over a period, wealth is a stock, and cash buffers affect short-term responses. Control over production and management, eligibility, relationships, and recognition are other resources again. They may correlate or separate; a single label cannot fill in all of a person's opportunities. [T-main]","Class and stratification, stigma and boundaries, intersectionality, elite reproduction, colonial labor, and poverty and mobility provide different entry points. Naming alone does not prove that a group has a common will. Yet labels can have real consequences when they actually enter employers' practices, service rules, or interactions. If a researcher's internal classification never reaches those stages, merely being ‘named’ cannot establish that the same effects were activated. [T-main]","Intersectionality is not the number of identities or the overlap of circles in a diagram. It requires examining how multiple sets of rules jointly exclude particular positions. Statistical interaction is a relationship in a model; institutional intersection concerns how rules act together; lived experience concerns people's situations and meanings. These forms of evidence are connected, but one should not substitute for the other two. [T-main]","Educational mobility must distinguish birth cohorts, applications, admission, graduation, and subsequent opportunities. A few successes do not prove all entry points are open; skill differences alone do not prove fair educational opportunities. Conversely, real selection barriers do not cancel the value of skills someone has acquired. Examining elite control requires tracing access, agendas, resources, and removal, not merely attaching labels based on occupation or qualifications. [T-main]","The welfare example in the source is a teaching construction. Regional mean income among those included in the statistics may genuinely rise, while undocumented people remain outside both statistics and applications, requiring narrower coverage claims. Do not erase observed improvement to emphasize omission, or treat absent people as beneficiaries because the mean improved. The costs of obtaining documentation, travel, time, and care are specific constraints, not verdicts on character or ability. [T-main]","Resources become personal opportunities only with conditions of timing, use, control, and execution. A wealthy household does not mean every member can decide on and use its resources; a firm's assets are not necessarily available to employees. Recognition, redistribution, and participation rights respectively ask whether one is respected, what one receives, and whether one can influence rules. None substitutes for the other two. [T-main]","Specific measurement reference: the U.S. Census Bureau's P60-282, released September 10, 2024, reports 2023 median household income of $80,610 in 2023 dollars, up 4% from 2022. The same page distinguishes changes across groups and explains that the measure is pretax money income excluding in-kind transfers. This is not every American's disposable income or wealth, and it does not identify the causes of group differences. [P-CENSUS]","The agency's accompanying article explains that household categories are defined by the person who owns or rents the home. A ‘median for a household category’ therefore does not assert that every member shares the same identity, resources, powers, or experience. The additional observation in this lesson is the gap between the statistical unit and personal opportunity; the statistical report is not evidence that institutions have become fair. [P-CENSUS-GROUP]","Relative and absolute gaps must be recorded separately. Mathematically, the ratio of the lower to the higher group can move closer to 1 while the monetary difference grows. Whether the aim concerns proportions, absolute shortfalls in living conditions, minimum guarantees, or control must be made public. One cannot select a favorable indicator and declare inequality resolved. [T-main]","Connections in the main text: §2.5 concerns the scope of evidence and inference; §5.5.1 examines aggregation; §9.7 retains material and meaning-related conditions; §§10.5–10.8 examine overlap, power, and continuation across circles; and Part XIV addresses allocation and responsibility. This lesson does not turn these connections into a personal identity scoring tool. [T-main]","The eight research entry points are: 22.01, class, stratification, and the distribution of capital—examine distinct resources; 22.02, status, stigma, and boundaries—trace where labels arrive; 22.03, gender, race, ethnicity, and intersectionality—examine exclusion through interacting rules; 22.04, elite and oligarchic reproduction—examine control and removal; 22.05, colonialism, slavery, and racialized labor—retain specific historical channels rather than group essences; 22.06, poverty, deprivation, and scarcity—list real constraints; 22.07, mobility, education, and inheritance—specify denominators at each stage; and 22.08, recognition, redistribution, and common life—do not substitute normative aims for one another. This is the book's research navigation, not a comprehensive empirical review of these fields. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["List flows, stocks, control, eligibility, relationships, and recognition as distinct resources; do not use identity to fill gaps in knowledge.","Trace the channels through which labels actually reach rules or interactions.","Separate the evidential tasks for statistical intersections, interacting institutions, and lived experience.","Check grouping, stages, omitted positions, and individuals' control over resources.","Report both relative and absolute indicators and their normative aims, retaining local improvements and coverage gaps."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Household income","leftText":"Income over a period measured at household level.","rightTitle":"Personal opportunity","rightText":"Still requires conditions of control, use, time, and execution."},{"leftTitle":"Statistical interaction","leftText":"A joint relationship within a model.","rightTitle":"Institutional intersection","rightText":"How multiple rules jointly exclude; this requires evidence of mechanisms."},{"leftTitle":"Group mean or median","leftText":"A summary of a group distribution.","rightTitle":"An individual's circumstances","rightText":"A group value cannot be assigned directly to a member."},{"leftTitle":"A narrowing relative gap","leftText":"The ratio moves closer to 1.","rightTitle":"A narrowing absolute gap","rightText":"A smaller monetary difference—a separate comparison."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample from the source: mean income rises while people excluded from statistics and applications remain omitted.","Teaching counterexample: assigning income and character labels to an individual from the median of a household category.","Source boundary: a researcher's internal identity label does not automatically reach employers or service rules."],"questions":["Can a group median be used to judge an individual?","Does a smaller relative gap also mean a smaller absolute gap?","Does the existence of omitted people negate observed improvement?","What determines whether a label has an effect?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-22/figures/d22-resources.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The U.S. report on 2023 household income, released in 2024","href":"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-282.html?os=win"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The U.S. report on 2023 household income, released in 2024","text":"The P60-282 release page reports 2023 median household income of $80,610 in 2023 dollars, and defines the measure as pretax money income excluding in-kind transfers. Changes differ across groups.","sourceLabel":"U.S. Census Bureau: Income in the United States: 2023，P60-282","sourceUrl":"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-282.html?os=win","sourceLocator":"Release page dated 2024-09-10: Introduction and Highlights items 1–4, web-page lines 202–212; For More Information, lines 303–310","boundary":"Limited to that historical version and the household unit. It does not measure individual control of resources, wealth, the latest current income, or the causes of group differences.","href":"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-282.html?os=win"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.22","start_line":5048,"end_line":5063,"startLine":5048,"endLine":5063}],"outputId":"section-d-22","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: relatively closer, absolutely farther apart","text":"Artificial lower-group/higher-group incomes change from 50/100 to 90/150. The ratio rises from 0.5 to 0.6, moving closer to 1, while the absolute gap grows from 50 to 60. The lower group's income itself also rises from 50 to 90. All three statements can be true simultaneously; selecting just one cannot establish that every aim has been achieved.","assumptions":"Fictional incomes for two groups using the same units and time basis; no causal data.","boundary":"This concerns no real identity group and does not imply that all inequality indicators move in the same direction."},{"title":"Teaching construction: total resources do not equal personal control","text":"A fictional two-person household has a total of 100, but one person can independently dispose of 10 while the other controls 90. The total of 100 may be accurate; a per-person average of 50 does not mean both can freely use 50. The distribution of control requires independent evidence.","assumptions":"An artificial allocation that represents no household or group convention.","boundary":"Do not infer coercion or responsibility from household type."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample from the source: mean income rises while people excluded from statistics and applications remain omitted.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: assigning income and character labels to an individual from the median of a household category.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: a researcher's internal identity label does not automatically reach employers or service rules.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can a group median be used to judge an individual?","answer":"No direct assignment is warranted; individual control over resources and specific evidence are needed.","basis":"Units and distributions."},{"question":"Does a smaller relative gap also mean a smaller absolute gap?","answer":"Not necessarily: the change from 50/100 to 90/150 is a counterexample.","basis":"Two different aims."},{"question":"Does the existence of omitted people negate observed improvement?","answer":"It does not negate a real local outcome, but coverage claims must be narrowed and omitted positions traced.","basis":"Positive and negative outcomes can coexist."},{"question":"What determines whether a label has an effect?","answer":"Check which rule or interaction it actually enters and what follows. An internal label is not an automatic intervention.","basis":"Channels."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-22/figures/d22-resources.png","alt":"Separate income, wealth, buffers, control, eligibility, relationships, and recognition; labels do not fill unknowns about individuals.","caption":"Based on D.22."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-22/figures/d22-statistics.png","alt":"The 2024 Census report concerns 2023 median pretax household money income; it cannot be turned into a measure of individual control.","caption":"The measure specified on the official release page, not an estimate of current income."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-22/figures/d22-gaps.png","alt":"Artificial change from 50/100 to 90/150: the ratio goes from 0.5 to 0.6, and the absolute gap from 50 to 60.","caption":"Teaching construction."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures show resource categories, the boundary between statistics and individuals, and an artificial calculation of relative and absolute gaps.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["The original CPS microdata, full PDF, and causes of group differences were not analyzed.","Research entry points do not constitute a claim to have read original works across all eight fields or a diagnosis of an individual's circumstances."],"taskId":"S-D-22","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.22, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4869–4884","claim_support":["Resources, labels, intersectionality, stage-specific denominators, omissions, relative and absolute gaps, and eight entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-CENSUS","title":"U.S. Census Bureau: Income in the United States: 2023，P60-282","url":"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-282.html?os=win","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2024-09-10; measured in 2023 dollars","locator":"Release page dated 2024-09-10: Introduction and Highlights items 1–4, web-page lines 202–212; For More Information, lines 303–310","claim_support":["The version released in 2024 reports 2023 real median household income of $80,610 in 2023 dollars, up 4% from 2022. Changes differ across household categories. The article defines income as pretax money income excluding in-kind transfers."],"limits":["The lesson author read the specified passages on the official release page, not the full PDF or microdata. This is the measure from that historical version, not the latest 2026 income or an individual's disposable amount; group differences are not used to infer individual circumstances or causes."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-CENSUS-GROUP","title":"U.S. Census Bureau: Median Income of Non-Hispanic White Households Increased While Asian, Black and Hispanic Median Household Income Did Not Change","url":"https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2024/09/household-income-race-hispanic.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Article dated 2024-09-10; page modified 2026-07-20","locator":"Article dated 2024-09-10, web-page lines 142–160, especially the definition of household categories at line 147","claim_support":["The article defines a household category by one person who owns or rents the home, rather than directly describing each household member's income."],"limits":["The lesson author read the article and does not report the SPM poverty rate mentioned in its update notice. Group statistics do not prove an individual's ability or character, the causes of discrimination, or a common will."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.22 Class, Identity, Inequality, and Social Exclusion; Chinese source lines 4957–4972; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-22/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-22/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-22/source/S-D-22.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-22/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"fc79ed339c8de7455dc19917a54455d1ddb7a13095af00868cd7446fbcd23bbf","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-22.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-22.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-22.md"};function d(u,e,h,m,p,g){const t=a("SectionLesson");return n(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-22" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.22</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Class, Identity, Inequality, and Social Exclusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-22" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.22 Class, Identity, Inequality, and Social Exclusion {#section-d-22}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the main text, see §2.5, §5.5.1, §9.7, §10.5–10.8, and §14.4–14.5. Research entries cover 22.01–22.08.</p><p>Income, wealth, cash buffers, control over production and management, qualifications, social ties, and standing through recognition are distinct resources. Research on class and strata, stigma and social boundaries, intersectionality, elite reproduction, colonialism and labor, poverty, and mobility points to different possible channels. An identity category alone cannot determine an individual’s circumstances.</p><p>Naming a group does not establish a common will. Naming can have real effects through classification, service eligibility, employment, and interaction. Intersectionality calls for examining how several sets of rules jointly exclude a particular position, in addition to overlap among circles or the number of identities. Statistical interactions, intersections among institutions, and lived experience require separate arguments. An improvement in a group average can coexist with continuing exclusion within the group.</p><p>Analysis of education and social mobility must distinguish birth cohort, enrollment applications, admission, graduation, and subsequent opportunities. Measured differences in skill do not establish fairness in opportunities to learn. The success of a few people does not represent the openness of the institution as a whole. Conversely, barriers to entry do not erase the value of actual skills. To establish elite control, trace access, agendas, resources, and mechanisms for removal. Educational credentials or occupations alone do not establish it.</p><p>In a teaching example, a benefit raises an area’s average income, but people without identity documents are excluded from the statistics and the application process. The average result can remain valid. Claims about coverage must be narrowed, and people who did not enter the process must be listed separately. The time, transport, care, and evidence-gathering costs that poverty brings should be recorded as concrete constraints. They must not become definitive judgments about capability or character. Recognition, redistribution, and participation rights each raise distinct questions.</p><p>Resources become opportunities for individuals through conditions of time, use, control, and implementation. Household totals, business assets, or group averages can answer aggregate questions. They cannot directly assign control and welfare to individuals. An identity label can have effects only through a channel by which it actually reaches an employer, service rule, or interaction. A label used only by researchers does not automatically activate those consequences. Relative gaps can shrink while absolute gaps widen. State the comparison objectives separately.</p><p>Research topics. 22.01 Class, strata, and the distribution of capital. 22.02 Standing, stigma, and social boundaries. 22.03 Gender, race, ethnicity, and intersectionality. 22.04 Elites, oligarchies, and reproduction of power. 22.05 Colonialism, slavery, and racialized labor. 22.06 Poverty, deprivation, and scarcity. 22.07 Social mobility, education, and inheritance. 22.08 Recognition, redistribution, and life together.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.22"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=i(l,[["render",d]]);export{b as __pageData,y as default};
